<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afrikaners</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afrikaner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Afrikaners are a South African people descended mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers, together with German and French Huguenot immigrants, who arrived after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> founded the Cape settlement in 1652. They developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> language from Dutch. They are one of the lasting legacies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> established a victualling station at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> in 1652 as a watering point for ships bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Company officials did not favour permanent European settlement, but supplying garrisons and passing fleets led the administration to free some of its employees and oblige them to farm independently. In 1657 the first of these free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, the vrijburgers, received farms, and the white population was only about 134. The company offered free passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> families from 1685 to 1707, and in 1688 it sponsored the settlement of 200 French Huguenot refugees, in the hope that people used to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> agriculture would succeed with vines and olives where the Dutch had not.</p><p>German soldiers returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> in company service added to the colonists. By 1691 over a quarter of the white population was not ethnically <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and a count at the start of British rule put 26,720 permanent settlers at 50 per cent Dutch, 27 per cent German, 17 per cent French and 5.5 per cent other. Their descendants also include a smaller number of Flemish, Danish, Norwegian, Swiss and Swedish settlers and of indigenous Africans. The first recorded marriage across lines of descent was in 1664 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, between a Khoi woman, Krotoa, and a Danish surgeon, Peder Havgaard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early colonists were called Christians, colonists, or ingezeetenen, inhabitants, and their sense of being rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, unlike the company's expatriate officials, found wide expression only in the late eighteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule at the Cape lasted about 140 years, and the colony became British after 1806. In the 20th century Afrikaner nationalism took the form of political parties and closed societies such as the Broederbond. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> was founded in 1914 to promote Afrikaner interests, won the general election of 1948 and enacted apartheid, a system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a>, and in 1961 declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> a republic. Afrikaners dominated the country's politics until the first non-racial elections of 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, the mother tongue of Afrikaners, evolved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialect of South Holland. The settlers came from Protestant Europe, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics were founded on the principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a>, whose congregations were centres of frontier communities. In 1985, 92 per cent of Afrikaners identified as members of the Reformed churches. The language is promoted by the Afrikaanse Taal en Kultuurvereniging, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> is a youth movement. Afrikaners are concentrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, especially around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, with a diaspora mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. About 100 black families who identify as Afrikaners live at Onverwacht, founded in 1886.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afrikaners” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second Boer War of 1899–1902 Boer commandos invaded the Cape, besieging Kimberley and Mafeking, and several thousand Cape <b>Afrikaners</b> joined them as rebels.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cape Colony, established in 1652 by Jan van Riebeeck as a VOC resupply station, produced the <b>Afrikaner</b> settler community whose descendants shaped South African history into the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic held a large place in the politics and memory of <b>Afrikaner</b> nationalism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A republic was the long-standing goal of the <b>Afrikaner</b> nationalist National Party, in power since 1948, and of its prime minister Hendrik Verwoerd, who won a referendum of white voters on 5 October 1960 with about 52 per cent of the vote.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its victory in 1881 showed that Britain could be resisted, and the survival of the republic became a symbol for Dutch-speaking whites throughout the region, feeding a growing <b>Afrikaner</b> national consciousness.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Vereeniging in 1902 the two Boer republics became British colonies, and in 1906–1907 they received responsible government, which brought the <b>Afrikaner</b> generals Louis Botha and Jan Smuts to power in the Transvaal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
